# 4. Task 2 - Reproduce the Archetypal Breach

## Objective

Deliberately create the most common real-world cloud storage mistake - a bucket policy with `Principal: "*"`. The goal is to understand exactly how it works so we can prevent it.

## Step 1 - Create the public bucket policy

```bash
cat > public-policy.json <<JSON
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Sid": "PublicReadEverything",
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:GetObject",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET/*"
  }]
}
JSON
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 6 - Public bucket policy JSON created](lab6_evidence_images/image6.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** The `cat > public-policy.json <<JSON` command wrote the dangerous policy. The policy allows `s3:GetObject` for `Principal: "*"` on all objects (`$BUCKET/*`).

## Step 2 - Apply and verify the policy

```bash
aws $EP s3api put-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET --policy file://public-policy.json
aws $EP s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET --query Policy --output text
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 7 - Public bucket policy applied and confirmed](lab6_evidence_images/image7.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `get-bucket-policy` confirmed `Principal: "*"`, `Effect: Allow`, `Action: s3:GetObject`, `Resource: arn:aws:s3:::miit-patient-records-18273/*`.

## Step 3 - Test anonymous access (the breach)

```bash
curl -s -o leaked.txt -w 'HTTP %{http_code}\n' \
  http://localhost:4566/$BUCKET/confidential/record.txt
cat leaked.txt
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 8 - Anonymous request exposed the confidential record](lab6_evidence_images/image8.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `curl` returned `HTTP 200`. `cat leaked.txt` printed: **"Patient: Ahmad bin Ali, Diagnosis: confidential"** - no AWS credentials were used at all.

## Result

The confidential record was fully exposed. There was **no exploit, no malware, no vulnerability** - the only cause was `"Principal": "*"`.

### Simple explanation

> `Principal: "*"` means **any entity in the world** - including anonymous internet users with no AWS account - can act as the principal. Combined with `s3:GetObject` on `$BUCKET/*`, anyone who knows the URL can read every object in the bucket.

