# 5. Task 3 - Remediate with Block Public Access

## Objective

Block Public Access is a **preventative guardrail** - it stops a public policy from being accepted in the first place, protecting against future mistakes by any team member.

## Steps

```bash
# 1. Remove the dangerous policy
aws $EP s3api delete-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET
# 2. Enable all four Block Public Access settings
aws $EP s3api put-public-access-block --bucket $BUCKET \
  --public-access-block-configuration \
  BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true
# 3. Verify the settings
aws $EP s3api get-public-access-block --bucket $BUCKET
# 4. Try to re-apply the public policy (guardrail should reject it on real AWS)
aws $EP s3api put-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET --policy file://public-policy.json
# 5. Re-test anonymous access
curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'anonymous read now: HTTP %{http_code}\n' \
  http://localhost:4566/$BUCKET/confidential/record.txt
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 9 - Block Public Access all four flags enabled](lab6_evidence_images/image9.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `get-public-access-block` confirmed all four settings are `true`: `BlockPublicAcls`, `IgnorePublicAcls`, `BlockPublicPolicy`, `RestrictPublicBuckets`. The anonymous `curl` re-test returned `HTTP 200` - LocalStack stores the config but does not always enforce it. On **real AWS**, `BlockPublicPolicy: true` would have rejected the `put-bucket-policy` call itself.

### Simple explanation

> **Guardrail vs Detective Control:**
> - A **detective control** detects a problem *after* it happens (e.g., AWS Config alerts when a bucket becomes public).
> - A **guardrail (preventative control)** stops the unsafe configuration from being created *in the first place*.
>
> For an organisation with many engineers, a guardrail is stronger - it prevents any single careless or mistaken action from exposing data, regardless of who makes it.

## Least-privilege policy

After the guardrail was applied, a least-privilege policy was created to grant access only to the `internal/` prefix:

```json
{
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::000000000000:root"},
    "Action": "s3:GetObject",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET/internal/*"
  }]
}
```

> Scoping to `internal/*` instead of `$BUCKET/*` means the confidential prefix is never automatically exposed.

