# 8. Task 6A - Presigned URL (Temporary Delegated Access)

A presigned URL gives **time-limited access** to a specific S3 object without requiring the recipient to have AWS credentials.

## Steps

```bash
URL=$(aws $EP s3 presign s3://$BUCKET/internal/roster.txt --expires-in 60)
echo "$URL"
curl -s -w '<- HTTP %{http_code}\n' "$URL"
sleep 65
curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'after expiry: HTTP %{http_code}\n' "$URL"
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 19 - Presigned URL generated and accessed successfully](lab6_evidence_images/image19.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** The presigned URL was generated with 60-second expiry (`X-Amz-Expires=60`). `curl` returned the content `Staff duty schedule, week 12` and `HTTP 200`.

![Evidence Image 20 - Presigned URL tested after the 60-second expiry](lab6_evidence_images/image20.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** After `sleep 65` seconds, `curl` returned `after expiry: HTTP 200`. Note: LocalStack does not always enforce presigned URL expiry. On real AWS this would return `403 Forbidden`.

### Simple explanation

> A presigned URL is a **temporary access token embedded in the URL** - anyone with it can access the object until it expires (or until the issuing credentials are revoked). It should be:
> - **Short-lived** (use the shortest appropriate expiry time)
> - **Limited to one specific object**
> - Used only when delegated access is genuinely needed

