# 9. Task 6B - `aws:SecureTransport` Condition-Key Trap

This policy denies all S3 access over plain HTTP, forcing HTTPS:

```json
{
  "Statement": [{
    "Sid": "DenyUnencryptedTransport",
    "Effect": "Deny",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:*",
    "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET", "arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET/*"],
    "Condition": { "Bool": { "aws:SecureTransport": "false" } }
  }]
}
```

## Steps

```bash
aws $EP s3api put-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET --policy file://secure-transport.json
aws $EP s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET --query Policy --output text
aws $EP s3api list-objects-v2 --bucket $BUCKET
aws $EP s3api delete-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET
aws $EP s3api get-bucket-policy --bucket $BUCKET --query Policy --output text
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 21 - SecureTransport Deny policy confirmed on bucket](lab6_evidence_images/image21.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `get-bucket-policy` returned the full `DenyUnencryptedTransport` policy with `Principal: "*"`, `Effect: Deny`, condition `aws:SecureTransport: "false"`.

![Evidence Image 22 - Bucket contents listed while SecureTransport policy was active](lab6_evidence_images/image22.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `list-objects-v2` returned the bucket contents (`confidential/record-v2.txt`, `confidential/record.txt`, `internal/roster.txt`). LocalStack returned data despite the HTTP transport and active Deny policy.

![Evidence Image 23 - SecureTransport policy removed, NoSuchBucketPolicy confirmed](lab6_evidence_images/image23.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** After `delete-bucket-policy`, `get-bucket-policy` returned: `NoSuchBucketPolicy - The bucket policy does not exist` - confirming the policy was removed.

### Simple explanation

> The `aws:SecureTransport` policy is **correct for production HTTPS** - it forces all S3 requests to use TLS/HTTPS. But LocalStack runs on HTTP (`localhost:4566`), so every request matches the Deny condition and would block everything.
>
> **Lesson:** Security conditions must be tested in the *actual environment* where they will run. A correct production condition can accidentally block everything in an HTTP-only simulation environment.

