## Part B - Cryptographic Erasure

Cryptographic erasure is the fastest and most reliable deletion method: **destroy the KMS key, and all ciphertext encrypted by it becomes permanently unreadable noise**.

## Steps - Cryptographic Erasure

```bash
# Check key state
aws $EP kms describe-key --key-id $KEY_ID \
  --query 'KeyMetadata.[KeyId,KeyState,Enabled]' --output text
# Disable the key
aws $EP kms disable-key --key-id $KEY_ID
# Check state again
aws $EP kms describe-key --key-id $KEY_ID \
  --query 'KeyMetadata.[KeyState,Enabled]' --output text
# Schedule deletion with 7-day window
aws $EP kms schedule-key-deletion --key-id $KEY_ID --pending-window-in-days 7
# Verify deletion date
aws $EP kms describe-key --key-id $KEY_ID \
  --query 'KeyMetadata.[KeyState,DeletionDate]' --output text
# Attempt to read encrypted data (should fail on real AWS)
aws $EP s3api get-object --bucket $BUCKET \
  --key confidential/record-v2.txt after-erasure.txt
```

### Evidence

![Evidence Image 30 - KMS key initially Enabled then Disabled](lab6_evidence_images/image30.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** First `describe-key` returned `Enabled True`. After `disable-key`, second `describe-key` returned `Disabled False` - the key is no longer usable for encrypt or decrypt operations.

![Evidence Image 31 - KMS key scheduled for deletion with 7-day window](lab6_evidence_images/image31.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `schedule-key-deletion` returned `DeletionDate: 2026-09-18T08:40:46.144994-07:00`, `KeyState: PendingDeletion`, `PendingWindowInDays: 7`. The subsequent `describe-key` confirmed `PendingDeletion` state.

![Evidence Image 32 - Object read after KMS key disable and scheduled deletion](lab6_evidence_images/image32.png)

**What the screenshot shows:** `get-object` returned the object with `ServerSideEncryption: aws:kms` and the full KMS Key ARN. LocalStack does not re-check KMS key state on every read. **On real AWS**, disabling the key causes `KMSDisabledException` and the read would fail completely.

### Simple explanation

> **Cryptographic erasure** works by destroying the decryption key:
>
> ```
> Disable/delete the KMS key
>     -> All objects encrypted by that key -> permanently unreadable ciphertext
>     -> Even physical access to the storage media cannot recover the data
> ```
>
> This provides **stronger deletion assurance than file overwriting** because the data owner does not control the cloud provider's physical storage, backups, or redundant replicas. Destroying the key makes all copies permanently useless at once.

